<section xml:id="linear07" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>The Trace-Determinant Plane</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>

            <li><p>To understand that the characteristic polynomial of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix can be written as 
                <md>\lambda^2 - T \lambda + D,</md>
            where <m>T = \trace(A)</m> and <m>D = \det(A)</m>.  Furthermore, if a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> has eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m>, then <m>\trace(A)</m> is <m>\lambda_1 + \lambda_2</m> and <m>\det(A) = \lambda_1 \lambda_2</m>, and the trace and determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix are invariant under a change of coordinates.</p></li>

            <li><p>To understand that the trace-determinant plane is determined by the graph of the parabola <m>D= T^2/4</m> on the <m>TD</m>-plane and that the trace-determinant plane can be used to determine  the phase portrait of a linear system.</p></li>

            <li><p>To understand that the trace-determinant plane is useful for studying bifurcations.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>    
    <introduction>

        <p>Suppose that we have two tanks, Tank <m>A</m> and Tank <m>B</m>, that both have a volume of <m>V</m> liters and are both filled with a brine solution. Suppose that pure water enters Tank <m>A</m> at a rate of <m>r_{\text{in}}</m> liters per minute, and a salt mixture enters Tank <m>A</m> from Tank <m>B</m> at a rate of <m>r_B</m> liters per minute. Brine also enters Tank <m>B</m> from Tank <m>A</m> at a rate of <m>r_A</m> liters per minute. Finally, brine is drained from Tank <m>B</m> at a rate of <m>r_{\text{out}}</m> so that the volume in each tank is constant (<xref ref="linear07-figure-mixing-example-trace-determinant"/>).</p>
<!--
        <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-mixing-example">
            <image width="60%" xml:id="linear07-mixing-example">
                <description>two tanks containing a brine solution with a pipe flowing into the first tank and two pipes connecting the tanks and a pipe draining the second tank</description>
                <latex-image>
                    <xi:include href="tikz/linear07-mixing-example.tex" parse="text"/>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
            <caption>Mixing example with two tanks</caption>
        </figure>
    -->

        <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-mixing-example-trace-determinant">
             <image width="60%">
                <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-mixing-example-trace-determinant.xml"/>
              </image>
            <caption>Mixing example with two tanks</caption>
        </figure>

        <p>If <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m> are the amounts of salt in Tank <m>A</m> and Tank <m>B</m>, respectively, then our problem can be modeled with a linear system of two equations,
            <md>
                <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp = \text{rate in} - \text{rate out} = - r_A \frac{x}{V} + r_B \frac{y}{V}</mrow>
                <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp = \text{rate in} - \text{rate out} = r_A \frac{x}{V} - r_B \frac{y}{V} - r_{\text{out}} \frac{y}{V}.</mrow>
            </md>
        Furthermore, <m>r_A = r_B + r_{\text{out}}</m>, since the volume in Tank <m>B</m> is constant. Consequently, our system now becomes
            <md>
                <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp = - r_A \frac{x}{V} + r_B \frac{y}{V}</mrow>
                <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp  = r_A \frac{x}{V} - r_A \frac{y}{V}.</mrow>
            </md>
       If we have initial conditions <m>x(0) = x_0</m> and <m>y(0) = y_0</m>, it is not too difficult to deduce that the amount of salt in each tank will approach zero as <m>t \to \infty</m>, and we will have a stable equilibrium solution at <m>(0, 0)</m>. Determining the nature of the equilibrium solution is a more difficult question. For example, is it ever possible that the equilibrium solution is a spiral sink? One solution is provided by studying the <term>trace-determinant plane</term><idx>trace-determinant plane</idx>.</p>

    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="linear07-subsection-trace-determinant">
        <title>The Trace-Determinant Plane</title>

        <p>The key to solving the system
            <md>\begin{pmatrix}
            x' \\ y'
            \end{pmatrix}
            =
            \begin{pmatrix}
            a &amp; b \\
            c &amp; d
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
            x \\ y
            \end{pmatrix}
            = 
            A
            \begin{pmatrix}
            x \\ y
            \end{pmatrix}</md>
        is determining the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>. To find these eigenvalues, we need to derive the characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m>,
            <md>\det(A - \lambda I)
            =
            \det
            \begin{pmatrix}
            a - \lambda &amp; b \\
            c &amp; d - \lambda
            \end{pmatrix}
            =
            \lambda^2 - (a + d) \lambda + (ad - bc).</md>
        Of course, <m>D = \det(A) = ad -bc</m> is the determinant of <m>A</m>. The quantity <m>T = a + d</m> is the sum of the diagonal elements of the matrix <m>A</m>. We call this quantity the <term>trace</term><idx><h>matrix</h><h>trace</h></idx> of <m>A</m> and write <m>\trace(A)</m>. Thus, we can rewrite the characteristic polynomial as
            <md>\det(A - \lambda I) = \lambda^2 - T \lambda + D.</md>
        We can use the trace and determinant to establish the nature of a solution to a linear system.</p>

        <theorem xml:id="linear07-theorem-trace-determinant">
            <statement>
                <p>If a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> has eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m>, then the trace of <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda_1 + \lambda_2</m> and <m>\det(A) = \lambda_1 \lambda_2</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <proof>
                <p>The proof follows from a direct computation. Indeed, we can rewrite the characteristic polynomial as
                    <md>\det(A - \lambda I) = \lambda^2 - T \lambda + D.</md> 
                The eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are now given by
                    <md>\lambda_1 = \frac{T + \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}}{2} \quad \text{and} \quad \lambda_2 = \frac{T - \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}}{2}.</md>
                Hence, <m>T = \lambda_1 + \lambda_2</m> and <m>D = \lambda_1 \lambda_2</m>.</p>
            </proof>
        </theorem> 

        <p><xref ref="linear07-theorem-trace-determinant" /> tells us that we can determine the determinant and trace of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix from its eigenvalues. Thus, we should be able to determine the phase portrait of a system <m>{\mathbf x}' = A {\mathbf x}</m> by simply examining the trace and determinant of <m>A</m>. Since the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are given by
            <md>\lambda = \frac{T \pm \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}}{2},</md>
        we can immediately see that the expression <m>T^2 - 4D</m> determines the nature of the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.
            <ul>

                <li><p>If <m>T^2 - 4D > 0</m>, we have two distinct real eigenvalues.</p></li>

                <li><p>If <m>T^2 - 4D \lt 0</m>, we have two complex eigenvalues, and these eigenvalues are complex conjugates.</p></li>

                <li><p>If <m>T^2 - 4D = 0</m>, we have repeated eigenvalues.</p></li>

            </ul>
        If <m>T^2 - 4D = 0</m> or equivalently if <m>D = T^2/4</m>, we have repeated eigenvalues. In fact, we can represent those systems with repeated eigenvalues by graphing the parabola <m>D= T^2/4</m> on the <m>TD</m>-plane or <term>trace-determinant plane</term> (<xref ref="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-plane" />). Therefore, points on the parabola correspond to systems with repeated eigenvalues, points above the parabola (<m>D \gt T^2/4</m> or equivalently <m>T^2 - 4D \lt 0</m>) correspond to systems with complex eigenvalues, and points below the parabola  (<m>D \lt T^2/4</m> or equivalently <m>T^2 - 4D  \gt 0</m>) correspond to systems with real eigenvalues.</p>


            <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-plane">
                 <image width="60%">
                    <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-trace-determinant-plane.xml"/>
                  </image>
                <caption>The trace-determinant plane</caption>
            </figure>


        <theorem xml:id="linear07-theorem-trace-determinant-coordinates">
            <statement>
                <p>The trace and determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix are invariant under a change of coordinates. That is, <m>\det(T^{-1} A T) = \det(A)</m> and <m>\trace(T^{-1} A T) = \trace(A)</m> for any <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> and any invertible <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>T</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <proof>
                <p>It is straightforward to verify that <m>\det(AB) = \det(A) \det(B)</m> and <m>\det(T^{-1}) = 1/\det(T)</m> for <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.  Therefore,
                    <md>\det(T^{-1} A T) = \det(T^{-1}) \det(A) \det(T) = \frac{1}{\det(T)} \det(A) \det(T) = \det(A).</md>
                A direct computation shows that <m>\trace(AB) = \trace(BA)</m>.  Thus,
                    <md>\trace(T^{-1} A T) = \trace (T^{-1} T A ) = \trace(A).</md></p>
            </proof>
        </theorem> 

        <p>Furthermore, the expression <m>T^2 - 4D</m> is not affected by a change of coordinates by <xref ref="linear07-theorem-trace-determinant-coordinates" />. That is, we only need to consider systems <m>{\mathbf x}' = A {\mathbf x}</m>, where <m>A</m> is one of the following matrices:
            <md>\begin{pmatrix}
            \alpha &amp;  \beta \\
            -\beta &amp; \alpha
            \end{pmatrix},
            \begin{pmatrix}
            \lambda &amp; 0 \\
            0 &amp; \mu
            \end{pmatrix},
            \begin{pmatrix}
            \lambda &amp; 0 \\
            0 &amp; \lambda
            \end{pmatrix},
            \begin{pmatrix}
            \lambda &amp; 1 \\
            0 &amp; \lambda
            \end{pmatrix}.</md></p>

        <p>The system 
            <md>{\mathbf x}' = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha &amp;  \beta \\ - \beta &amp; \alpha \end{pmatrix} {\mathbf x}</md>
        has eigenvalues <m>\lambda = \alpha \pm i \beta</m>. The general solution to this system is
            <md>{\mathbf x}(t) 
            =
            c_1
            e^{\alpha t} 
            \begin{pmatrix}
            \cos \beta t \\ - \sin \beta t
            \end{pmatrix}
            +
            c_2 e^{\alpha t}
            \begin{pmatrix}
            \sin \beta t \\ \cos \beta t
            \end{pmatrix}.</md>
        The <m>e^{\alpha t}</m> factor tells us that the solutions either spiral into the origin if <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>, spiral out to infinity if <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>, or stay in a closed orbit if <m>\alpha = 0</m>. The equilibrium points are <term>spiral sinks</term><idx><h>equilibrium solution</h><h>spiral sink</h></idx> and <term>spiral sources</term><idx><h>equilibrium solution</h><h>spiral source</h></idx>, or <term>centers</term><idx><h>equilibrium solution</h><h>center</h></idx>, respectively.</p>

        <p>The eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are given by
            <md>\lambda = \frac{T \pm \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}}{2}.</md> 
        If <m>T^2 - 4D \lt 0</m>, then we have a complex eigenvalues, and the type of equilibrium point depends on the real part of the eigenvalue.  The sign of the real part is determined solely by <m>T</m>.  If <m>T \gt 0</m> we have a source.  If <m>T \lt 0</m>, we have a sink.  If <m>T = 0</m>, we have a center.  See <xref ref="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-complex" />.</p>

            <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-complex">
                 <image width="60%">
                    <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-trace-determinant-complex.xml"/>
                  </image>
                <caption><m>D \gt T^2/4</m></caption>
            </figure>

        <p>The situation for distinct real eigenvalues is a bit more complicated. Suppose that we have a system
            <md>{\mathbf x}'
            =
            \begin{pmatrix}
            \lambda &amp; 0 \\
            0 &amp; \mu
            \end{pmatrix}
             {\mathbf x}</md>
        with distinct eigenvalues <m>\lambda</m> and <m>\mu</m>. We will have three cases to consider if none of our eigenvalues are zero:
            <ul>

                <li>Both eigenvalues are positive (source).</li>

                <li>Both eigenvalues are negative (sink).</li>

                <li>One eigenvalue is negative and the other is positive (saddle).</li>

            </ul>
        Our two eigenvalues are given by
            <md>\lambda = \frac{T \pm \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}}{2}.</md></p>

        <p>If <m>T \gt 0</m>, then the eigenvalue
            <md>\frac{T + \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}}{2}</md>
        is positive and we need only determine the sign of the second eigenvalue
            <md>\frac{T - \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}}{2}</md>
        If <m>D \lt 0</m>, we have one positive and one zero eigenvalue. That is, we have a <term>saddle</term><idx><h>equilibrium solution</h><h>saddle</h></idx> if <m>T \gt 0</m> and <m>D \lt 0</m>.</p>

        <p>If <m>D \gt 0</m>, then
            <md>T^2 - 4D \lt T^2.</md>
        Since we are considering the case <m>T \gt 0</m>, we have 
            <md>\sqrt{T^2 - 4D} \lt T</md>
        and the value of the second eigenvalue <m>(T - \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}\,)/2</m> is postive. Therefore, any point in the first quadrant below the parabola corresponds to a system with two positive eigenvalues and must correspond to a <term>nodal source</term><idx><h>equilibrium solution</h><h>nodal source</h></idx>.</p>

        <p>One the other hand, suppose that <m>T \lt 0</m>. Then the eigenvalue <m>(T - \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}\,)/2</m> is always negative, and we need to determine if other eigenvalue is positive or negative.  If <m>D \lt 0</m>, then <m>T^2 - 4D \gt T^2</m> and <m>\sqrt{T^2 - 4D} \gt T</m>. Therefore, the other eigenvalue <m>(T - \sqrt{T^2 - 4D}\,)/2</m> is positive, telling us that any point in the fourth quadrant must correspond to a saddle. If <m>D \gt 0</m>, then <m>\sqrt{T^2 - 4D} \lt T</m> and the second eigenvalue is negative. In this case, we will have a <term>nodal sink</term><idx><h>equilibrium solution</h><h>nodal sink</h></idx>. We summarize our findings in <xref ref="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-real" />.</p>

            <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-real">
                 <image width="60%">
                    <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-trace-determinant-real.xml"/>
                  </image>
                 <caption>The trace-determinant plane for real and complex eigenvalues</caption>
            </figure>

        <p>For repeated eigenvalues, the analysis depends only on <m>T</m>.  Since
            <md>T^2 - 4D = 0,</md>
        the only eigenvalue is <m>T/2</m>. Thus, we have sources if <m>T > 0</m> and sinks if <m>T \lt 0</m> (<xref ref="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-repeated" />).</p>

            <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-trace-determinant-repeated">
                 <image width="60%">
                    <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-trace-determinant-repeated.xml"/>
                  </image>
                 <caption><m>D = T^2/4</m></caption>
            </figure>

        <example>
            <p>Let us return to the mixing problem that we proposed at the beginning of this section. The problem could be modeled by the system of equations
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp = - r_A \frac{x}{V} + r_B \frac{y}{V}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp  = r_A \frac{x}{V} - r_A \frac{y}{V}</mrow>
                    <mrow>x(0) \amp = x_0</mrow>
                    <mrow>y(0) \amp = y_0.</mrow>
                </md>
            The matrix corresponding to this system is
                <md>A = 
                \begin{pmatrix} -r_A/V \amp + r_B/V \\
                r_A / V \amp - r_A / V
                \end{pmatrix}.</md>
            Computing the trace and determinant of the matrix yields <m>T = - 2 r_A/V</m> and <m>D = (r_A^2 - r_A r_B)/V^2</m>, where <m>r_A</m> and <m>r_B</m> are both positive. Certainly, <m>T \lt 0</m> and
                <md>D = \frac{r_A^2 - r_A r_B}{V^2} = \frac{r_A(r_A - r_B)}{V^2} = \frac{r_A r_{\text{out}}}{V^2} \gt 0.</md>
            Therefore, any solution must be stable. Finally, since
                <md>4D - T^2 = 4 \frac{r_A^2 - r_A r_B}{V^2} - \left( \frac{-2 r_A}{V} \right)^2 = -\frac{4r_A r_B}{V^2} \lt 0,</md>
            we are below the parabola in the trace-determinant plane and know that our solution must be a nodal sink.</p>
        </example>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="linear07-subsection-parameterized-families">
        <title>Parameterized Families of Linear Systems</title>

        <p>The trace-determinant plane is an example of a <term>parameter plane</term><idx>parameter plane</idx>. We can adjust the entries of a matrix <m>A</m> and, thus, change the value of the trace and the determinant.</p>

        <p>Recall that a harmonic oscillator can be modeled by the second-order equation
            <md>m \frac{d^2 x}{dt^2} + b \frac{dx}{dt} + k x = 0,</md>
        where <m>m > 0</m> is the mass, <m>b \geq 0</m> is the damping coefficient, and <m>k \gt 0</m> is the spring constant. If we rewrite this equation as a first-order system, we have
            <md>{\mathbf x}'
            =
            \begin{pmatrix}
            0 &amp; 1 \\
            -k/m &amp; - b/m
            \end{pmatrix}
            {\mathbf x}.</md>
        Thus, for the harmonic oscillator <m>T = -b/m</m> and <m>D= k/m</m>. If we use the trace-determinant plane to analyze the harmonic oscillator, we need only concern ourselves with the second quadrant (Figure <xref ref="linear07-figure-one-parameter-harmonic" />).</p>

            <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-one-parameter-harmonic">
                 <image width="60%">
                    <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-one-parameter-harmonic.xml"/>
                  </image>
                <caption>A one-parameter family for a harmonic oscillator</caption>
            </figure>



        <p>If <m>(T, D) = (-b/m, k/m)</m> lies above the parabola, we have an underdamped oscillator. If <m>(T, D) = (-b/m, k/m)</m> lies below the parabola, we have an overdamped oscillator. If <m>(T, D) = (-b/m, k/m)</m> lies on the parabola, we have a critically damped oscillator. If <m>b = 0</m>, we have an undamped oscillator.</p>

        <example>
            <p>Now let us see what happens to our harmonic oscillator when we fix <m>m = 1</m> and <m>k = 3</m> and let the damping <m>b</m> vary between zero and infinity. We can rewrite our system as
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} &amp; = y</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} &amp; = - 3x - by.</mrow>
                </md>
            Thus, <m>T = -b</m> and <m>D = 3</m>.  We can see how the phase portrait varies with the parameter <m>b</m> in Figure  <xref ref="linear07-figure-varying-damping" />.</p>

            <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-varying-damping">
                 <image width="60%">
                        <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-varying-damping.xml"/>
                  </image>
                <caption>The trace-determinant plane for varying damping</caption>
            </figure>

            <p>The line <m>D = 3</m> in the trace-determinant plane crosses the repeated eigenvalue parabola, <m>D = T^2/4</m> if <m>b^2 = 12</m> or when <m>b = 2 \sqrt{3}</m>. If <m>b = 0</m>, we have purely imaginary eigenvalues.  This is the undamped harmonic oscillator. If <m>0 \lt b \lt 2 \sqrt{3}</m>, the eigenvalues are complex with a nonzero real part<mdash />the underdamped case. If <m>b = 2 \sqrt{3}</m>, the eigenvalues are negative and repeated<mdash />the critically damped case. Finally, if <m>b \gt 2 \sqrt{3}</m>, we have the overdamped case. In this case, the eigenvalues are real, distinct, and negative. A <terminology>bifurcation</terminology> occurs at <m>b = 2 \sqrt{3}</m>.</p>
        </example>
       
        <activity>
            <title>Harmonic Oscillator with a Varying Spring Constant</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>Consider a harmonic oscillator modeled by the second-order equation
		            <md number="yes" xml:id="linear07-equation-harmonic-activity">m \frac{d^2 x}{dt^2} + b \frac{dx}{dt} + k x = 0,</md>
		        where <m>m = 2</m> is the mass, <m>b = 2</m> is the damping coefficient, and <m>k \gt 0</m> is the spring constant. </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>Rewrite <xref ref="linear07-equation-harmonic-activity" /> as a system of first-order differential equations, <m>d\mathbf x/dt = A \mathbf x</m>.</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>Calculate the trace and determinant of <m>A</m>.</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>Sketch a line in the trace-determinant plane that parameterizes the family of equations <m>d\mathbf x/dt = A \mathbf x</m>.</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>For what values of <m>k</m> is the harmonic oscillator underdamped?  Overdamped?  For what value of <m>k</m> do we have a bifurcation?</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>

        <example>
            <p>Consider the system
                <md>\begin{pmatrix}
                x' \\ y'
                \end{pmatrix}
                = A \mathbf x
                = \begin{pmatrix}
                -2 &amp; a \\
                -2 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}
                {\mathbf x}.</md>
            The trace of <m>A</m> is always <m>T = -2</m>, but <m>D = \det(A) = 2a</m>. We are on the parabola if 
                <md>T^2 - 4D = 4 - 8a = 0 \qquad \text{or}\qquad a = \frac{1}{2}.</md>
            Thus, a bifurcation occurs at <m>a = 1/2</m>.  If <m>a \gt 1/2</m>, we have a spiral sink. If <m>a \lt 1/2</m>, we have a sink with real eigenvalues. Further more, if <m>a \lt 0</m>, our sink becomes a saddle (<xref ref="linear07-figure-one-parameter-family" />).</p>

            <figure xml:id="linear07-figure-one-parameter-family">
                 <image width="60%">
                    <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-one-parameter-family.xml"/>
                  </image>
                <caption>A one-parameter family of linear systems</caption>
            </figure>


	    </example>


       
        <activity>
            <title>Parameterized Families of Linear Systems</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>Consider the parameterized system of linear differential equations <m>d\mathbf x/dt = A \mathbf x</m>, where
                    <md>A  = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha \amp \beta \\ 1 \amp \alpha \end{pmatrix}.</md></p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>Find the trace, <m>T</m>, and determinant, <m>D</m>, of <m>A</m>.</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>Calculate <m>T^2 = 4D</m>.</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>For what values of <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m> is the origin a spiral sink of <m>d\mathbf x/dt = A \mathbf x</m>?  A spiral source?  A center?</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>For what values of <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m> is the origin a nodal sink of <m>d\mathbf x/dt = A \mathbf x</m>?  A nodal source?  A saddle?</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>Identify all of the regions in the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane where the system <m>d\mathbf x/dt = A \mathbf x</m> possesses a saddle, a sink, a spiral sink, and so on.  Plot your results on the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane.</p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>

        <example>
            <p>Although the trace-determinant plane gives us a great deal of information about our system, we can not determine everything from this parameter plane. For example, the matrices
                <md>A 
                =
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; 1 \\
                -1 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}
                \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                B =
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; -1 \\
                1 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}</md>
            both have the same trace and determinant, but the solutions to <m>{\mathbf x}' = A {\mathbf x}</m> wind around the origin in a clockwise direction while those of <m>{\mathbf x}' = B{\mathbf x}</m> wind around in a counterclockwise direction.</p>
        </example>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="linear07-subsection-important-lessons">
        <title>Important Lessons</title>
        
        <p><ul>
        
            <li><p>The characteristic polynomial of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix can be written as 
                <md>\lambda^2 - T \lambda + D,</md>
            where <m>T = \trace(A)</m> and <m>D = \det(A)</m>.</p></li>

            <li><p>If a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> has eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m>, then <m>\trace(A)</m> is <m>\lambda_1 + \lambda_2</m> and <m>\det(A) = \lambda_1 \lambda_2</m>.</p></li>


            <li><p>The trace and determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix are invariant under a change of coordinates.</p></li>

            <li><p>The trace-determinant plane is separated by the graph of the parabola <m>D= T^2/4</m> on the <m>TD</m>-plane. Points on the trace-determinant plane correspond to the trace and determinant of a linear system <m>{\mathbf x}' = A {\mathbf x}</m>. Since the trace and the determinant of a matrix determine the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>, we can use the trace-determinant plane to parameterize the phase portraits of linear systems.</p></li>

            <li><p>The trace-determinant plane is useful for studying bifurcations.</p></li>

        </ul></p>
                
    </subsection>

    <reading-questions xml:id="reading-questions-linear07">

        <exercise xml:id="reading-questions-linear07-1" label="rq-linear07-matrix-trace">
            <statement>
                <p>What is the trace of a matrix?</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="reading-questions-linear07-2" label="rq-linear07-trace-determinant-plane">
            <statement>
                <p>Explain what information the trace-determinant plane provides about a <m>2 \times 2</m> linear system.</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
        </exercise>

    </reading-questions>

    <exercises xml:id="linear07-exercises" filenamebase="linear07">
    <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="linear07-exercises-trace-determinant">
            <title>Classifiying Equilibrium Points</title>
            <introduction><p>Classify the equilibrium points of the system <m>\mathbf x' = A \mathbf x</m> based on the position of <m>(T, D)</m> in the trace-determinant plane in <xref ref="linear07-exercises-trace-determinant"/>. Sketch the phase portrait by hand and then use <em>Sage</em> to verify your result.</p></introduction>
             <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    1 \amp 2 \\
                    3 \amp 4
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\3&amp;4\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = 5, \qquad
                        D = \det(A) = 4 - 6 = -2.</md>
                  Since <m>D \lt 0</m>, the point <m>(T, D) = (5, -2)</m> lies below
                  the horizontal axis in the trace-determinant plane. This region
                  corresponds to real eigenvalues of opposite sign, so the equilibrium
                  at the origin is an <em>unstable saddle</em>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The eigenvalues are
                    <md>\lambda = \frac{5 \pm \sqrt{25 + 8}}{2}
                               = \frac{5 \pm \sqrt{33}}{2},</md>
                  giving <m>\lambda_1 = \tfrac{5+\sqrt{33}}{2} \approx 4.37</m>
                  (positive) and
                  <m>\lambda_2 = \tfrac{5-\sqrt{33}}{2} \approx -0.37</m> (negative).
                  The corresponding eigenvectors are
                    <md><mrow>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix}4\\3+\sqrt{33}\end{pmatrix}
                        \approx \begin{pmatrix}4\\8.74\end{pmatrix},</mrow>
                        <mrow>\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix}4\\3-\sqrt{33}\end{pmatrix}
                        \approx \begin{pmatrix}4\\-2.74\end{pmatrix}.</mrow></md>
                  The general solution is
                    <md>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1 e^{\lambda_1 t}\mathbf{v}_1
                                      + c_2 e^{\lambda_2 t}\mathbf{v}_2.</md>
                  Solutions on the stable manifold (along <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>) decay
                  to the origin; all other solutions diverge along directions
                  asymptotically parallel to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.
                  </p>

                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-1.xml"/>
                    </image>

                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    4 \amp 2 \\
                    3 \amp 2
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}4&amp;2\\3&amp;2\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = 6, \qquad
                        D = \det(A) = 8 - 6 = 2.</md>
                  Since <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>T \gt 0</m>, and the discriminant
                  <m>T^2 - 4D = 36 - 8 = 28 \gt 0</m>, the point
                  <m>(T, D) = (6, 2)</m> lies above the <m>T</m>-axis and above the
                  parabola <m>D = T^2/4</m> in the trace-determinant plane. This
                  region corresponds to two distinct positive real eigenvalues, so
                  the equilibrium at the origin is an <em>unstable node</em>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The eigenvalues are
                    <md>\lambda = \frac{6 \pm \sqrt{28}}{2} = 3 \pm \sqrt{7},</md>
                  giving <m>\lambda_1 = 3 + \sqrt{7} \approx 5.65</m> and
                  <m>\lambda_2 = 3 - \sqrt{7} \approx 0.35</m>, both positive.
                  The corresponding eigenvectors are
                    <md><mrow>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix}2\\\sqrt{7}-1\end{pmatrix}
                        \approx \begin{pmatrix}2\\1.65\end{pmatrix},</mrow>
                        <mrow>\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix}2\\-(\sqrt{7}+1)\end{pmatrix}
                        \approx \begin{pmatrix}2\\-3.65\end{pmatrix}.</mrow></md>
                  The general solution is
                    <md>\mathbf{x}(t)
                        = c_1 e^{(3+\sqrt{7})t}\mathbf{v}_1
                        + c_2 e^{(3-\sqrt{7})t}\mathbf{v}_2.</md>
                  All solutions diverge from the origin; trajectories leave tangent
                  to <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> (the slower direction) and become
                  asymptotically parallel to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> as
                  <m>t \to \infty</m>.
                  </p>

                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-2.xml"/>
                    </image>

                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    -3 \amp -8 \\
                    4 \amp -6
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}-3&amp;-8\\4&amp;-6\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md><mrow>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = -9,</mrow>
                        <mrow>D = \det(A) = (-3)(-6) - (-8)(4) = 50.</mrow></md>
                  Since <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>T \lt 0</m>, and the discriminant
                  <m>T^2 - 4D = 81 - 200 = -119 \lt 0</m>, the point
                  <m>(T, D) = (-9, 50)</m> lies above the parabola
                  <m>D = T^2/4</m> with <m>T \lt 0</m> in the trace-determinant
                  plane. This region corresponds to complex eigenvalues with negative
                  real part, so the equilibrium at the origin is a
                  <em>stable spiral</em>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The eigenvalues are
                    <md>\lambda = \frac{-9 \pm \sqrt{-119}}{2}
                               = -\frac{9}{2} \pm \frac{\sqrt{119}}{2}\,i,</md>
                  with real part <m>\alpha = -9/2 \lt 0</m> and angular frequency
                  <m>\beta = \sqrt{119}/2 \approx 5.45</m>. All solutions spiral
                  toward the origin as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
                  </p>

                     <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-3.xml"/>
                    </image>



                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    4 \amp -5 \\
                    3 \amp 2
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}4&amp;-5\\3&amp;2\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md><mrow>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = 6,</mrow>
                        <mrow>D = \det(A) = (4)(2) - (-5)(3) = 23.</mrow></md>
                  Since <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>T \gt 0</m>, and the discriminant
                  <m>T^2 - 4D = 36 - 92 = -56 \lt 0</m>, the point
                  <m>(T, D) = (6, 23)</m> lies above the parabola <m>D = T^2/4</m>
                  with <m>T \gt 0</m> in the trace-determinant plane. This region
                  corresponds to complex eigenvalues with positive real part, so the
                  equilibrium at the origin is an <em>unstable spiral</em>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The eigenvalues are
                    <md>\lambda = \frac{6 \pm \sqrt{-56}}{2}
                               = 3 \pm i\sqrt{14},</md>
                  with real part <m>\alpha = 3 \gt 0</m> and angular frequency
                  <m>\beta = \sqrt{14} \approx 3.74</m>. All non-trivial solutions
                  spiral away from the origin as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
                  </p>


                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-4.xml"/>
                    </image>



                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    -11 \amp 10 \\
                    4 \amp -5
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}-11&amp;10\\4&amp;-5\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md><mrow>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = -16,</mrow>
                        <mrow>D = \det(A) = (-11)(-5) - (10)(4) = 15.</mrow></md>
                  Since <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>T \lt 0</m>, and the discriminant
                  <m>T^2 - 4D = 256 - 60 = 196 \gt 0</m>, the point
                  <m>(T, D) = (-16, 15)</m> lies above the <m>T</m>-axis and below
                  the parabola <m>D = T^2/4</m> with <m>T \lt 0</m> in the
                  trace-determinant plane. This region corresponds to two distinct
                  negative real eigenvalues, so the equilibrium at the origin is a
                  <em>stable node</em>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The characteristic equation is
                    <md>\lambda^2 + 16\lambda + 15 = (\lambda+1)(\lambda+15) = 0,</md>
                  giving eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1 = -1</m> and
                  <m>\lambda_2 = -15</m>, both negative. The corresponding
                  eigenvectors are
                    <md>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
                        \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix}5\\-2\end{pmatrix}.</md>
                  The general solution is
                    <md>\mathbf{x}(t)
                        = c_1 e^{-t}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}
                        + c_2 e^{-15t}\begin{pmatrix}5\\-2\end{pmatrix}.</md>
                  All solutions decay to the origin; since <m>\lambda_2 = -15</m>
                  decays much faster, trajectories approach the origin tangent to the
                  dominant eigenvector direction <m>\mathbf{v}_1 = (1,1)^T</m>.
                  </p>
                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-5.xml"/>
                    </image>



                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    5 \amp -3 \\
                    -8 \amp -6
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}5&amp;-3\\-8&amp;-6\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md><mrow>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = -1,</mrow>
                        <mrow>D = \det(A) = (5)(-6) - (-3)(-8) = -54.</mrow></md>
                  Since <m>D \lt 0</m>, the point <m>(T, D) = (-1, -54)</m> lies
                  below the horizontal axis in the trace-determinant plane. This
                  region corresponds to real eigenvalues of opposite sign, so the
                  equilibrium at the origin is an <em>unstable saddle</em>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The eigenvalues are
                    <md>\lambda = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 + 216}}{2}
                               = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{217}}{2},</md>
                  giving <m>\lambda_1 = \tfrac{-1+\sqrt{217}}{2} \approx 6.87</m>
                  (positive) and
                  <m>\lambda_2 = \tfrac{-1-\sqrt{217}}{2} \approx -7.87</m>
                  (negative). The corresponding eigenvectors are
                    <md><mrow>\mathbf{v}_1
                        = \begin{pmatrix}6\\11-\sqrt{217}\end{pmatrix}
                        \approx \begin{pmatrix}6\\-3.73\end{pmatrix},</mrow>
                        <mrow>\mathbf{v}_2
                        = \begin{pmatrix}6\\11+\sqrt{217}\end{pmatrix}
                        \approx \begin{pmatrix}6\\25.73\end{pmatrix}.</mrow></md>
                  Solutions on the stable manifold (along <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>)
                  approach the origin; all other solutions are eventually repelled
                  along directions parallel to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.
                  </p>

                 
                <image width="60%">
                 <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-6.xml"/>
                </image>


                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    4 \amp -15 \\
                    3 \amp -8
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
              <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}4&amp;-15\\3&amp;-8\end{pmatrix}</m>,
              the trace and determinant are
                <md><mrow>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = -4,</mrow>
                    <mrow>D = \det(A) = (4)(-8) - (-15)(3) = 13.</mrow></md>
              Since <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>T \lt 0</m>, and the discriminant
              <m>T^2 - 4D = 16 - 52 = -36 \lt 0</m>, the point
              <m>(T, D) = (-4, 13)</m> lies above the parabola <m>D = T^2/4</m>
              with <m>T \lt 0</m> in the trace-determinant plane. This region
              corresponds to complex eigenvalues with negative real part, so the
              equilibrium at the origin is a <em>stable spiral</em>.
              </p>

              <p>The eigenvalues are
                <md>\lambda = \frac{-4 \pm \sqrt{-36}}{2} = -2 \pm 3i,</md>
              with real part <m>\alpha = -2 \lt 0</m> and angular frequency
              <m>\beta = 3</m>. All solutions spiral toward the origin as
              <m>t \to \infty</m>.
              </p>

            <image width="60%">
             <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-7.xml"/>
            </image>


            </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    4 \amp 11 \\
                    -8 \amp -3
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}4&amp;11\\-8&amp;-3\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md><mrow>T = \operatorname{tr}(A) = 1,</mrow>
                        <mrow>D = \det(A) = (4)(-3) - (11)(-8) = 76.</mrow></md>
                  Since <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>T \gt 0</m>, and the discriminant
                  <m>T^2 - 4D = 1 - 304 = -303 \lt 0</m>, the point
                  <m>(T, D) = (1, 76)</m> lies above the parabola <m>D = T^2/4</m>
                  with <m>T \gt 0</m> in the trace-determinant plane. This region
                  corresponds to complex eigenvalues with positive real part, so the
                  equilibrium at the origin is an <em>unstable spiral</em>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The eigenvalues are
                    <md>\lambda = \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{-303}}{2}
                               = \frac{1}{2} \pm \frac{\sqrt{303}}{2}\,i,</md>
                  with real part <m>\alpha = \tfrac{1}{2} \gt 0</m> and angular
                  frequency <m>\beta = \tfrac{\sqrt{303}}{2} \approx 8.72</m>. All
                  non-trivial solutions spiral away from the origin as
                  <m>t \to \infty</m>.
                  </p>

                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-8.xml"/>
                    </image>



                </solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>

        <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="linear07-exercises-bifurcation">
            <title>One-Parameter Families and Bifurcations</title>
            <introduction><p>Each of the following matrices in <xref ref="linear07-exercises-bifurcation"/> describes a family of differential equations <m>\mathbf x' = A \mathbf x</m> that depends on the parameter <m>\alpha</m>. For each one-parameter family sketch the curve in the trace-determinant plane determined by <m>\alpha</m>. Identify any values of <m>\alpha</m> where the type of system changes. These values are <term>bifurcation values</term><idx>bifurcation value</idx> of <m>\alpha</m>.</p></introduction>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    \alpha \amp 3 \\
                    -1 \amp 0
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For
                  <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&amp;3\\-1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = \alpha, \qquad D = 3.</md>
                  As <m>\alpha</m> varies, the point <m>(T,D) = (\alpha, 3)</m>
                  traces the <em>horizontal line <m>D = 3</m></em> in the
                  trace-determinant plane. The discriminant is
                  <m>T^2 - 4D = \alpha^2 - 12</m>.
                  </p>

                  <p>The type of equilibrium changes at the following values of
                  <m>\alpha</m>:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -2\sqrt{3}</m>: the point crosses the parabola
                    <m>D = T^2/4</m> from below; transition from
                    <em>stable node</em> to <em>stable spiral</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: <m>T = 0</m> with <m>D \gt 0</m>;
                    the equilibrium is a <em>center</em> (purely imaginary
                    eigenvalues). This is a bifurcation: the stability changes from
                    stable to unstable.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 2\sqrt{3}</m>: the point crosses the parabola
                    <m>D = T^2/4</m> again; transition from
                    <em>unstable spiral</em> to <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  The complete classification is:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \lt -2\sqrt{3}</m>: stable node.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -2\sqrt{3}</m>: stable improper node.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>-2\sqrt{3} \lt \alpha \lt 0</m>: stable spiral.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: center (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>0 \lt \alpha \lt 2\sqrt{3}</m>: unstable spiral.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 2\sqrt{3}</m>: unstable improper node.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \gt 2\sqrt{3}</m>: unstable node.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  </p>

                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-9.xml"/>
                    </image>


                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    \alpha \amp 3 \\
                    \alpha \amp 0
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For
                  <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&amp;3\\\alpha&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = \alpha, \qquad D = (α)(0) - (3)(\alpha) = -3\alpha.</md>
                  As <m>\alpha</m> varies, the point <m>(T, D) = (\alpha, -3\alpha)</m>
                  traces the <em>line <m>D = -3T</m></em> in the trace-determinant
                  plane, passing through the origin with slope <m>-3</m>.
                  The discriminant is
                    <md>T^2 - 4D = \alpha^2 + 12\alpha = \alpha(\alpha + 12).</md>
                  </p>

                  <p>The type of equilibrium changes at the following values of
                  <m>\alpha</m>:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: <m>D = 0</m>, so the origin is not an
                    isolated equilibrium. This is a bifurcation point where the nature
                    of the system changes fundamentally.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -12</m>: <m>T^2 - 4D = 0</m> (the curve meets
                    the parabola); transition from stable node to stable spiral.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  The complete classification is:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \lt -12</m>: <m>T \lt 0</m>, <m>D \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>\Delta \gt 0</m> — <em>stable node</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -12</m>: <m>T \lt 0</m>, <m>D \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>\Delta = 0</m> — <em>stable improper node</em>
                    (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>-12 \lt \alpha \lt 0</m>: <m>T \lt 0</m>,
                    <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>\Delta \lt 0</m> — <em>stable
                    spiral</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: <m>D = 0</m> — <em>degenerate</em>;
                    the origin is not an isolated equilibrium (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \gt 0</m>: <m>T \gt 0</m>, <m>D \lt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable saddle</em>.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  </p>

                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-10.xml"/>
                    </image>


                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    \alpha \amp 2 \\
                    \alpha \amp \alpha
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For
                  <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&amp;2\\\alpha&amp;\alpha\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = 2\alpha, \qquad D = \alpha^2 - 2\alpha.</md>
                  Eliminating <m>\alpha = T/2</m> gives
                    <md>D = \frac{T^2}{4} - T,</md>
                  so the curve is a <em>parabola shifted downward</em> from the
                  standard parabola <m>D = T^2/4</m> by <m>T</m>. The discriminant
                  along the curve is
                    <md>T^2 - 4D = T^2 - 4\!\left(\frac{T^2}{4} - T\right) = 4T = 8\alpha.</md>
                  </p>

                  <p>The curve crosses key boundaries at:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m> (<m>T=0</m>, <m>D=0</m>): the origin
                    of the trace-determinant plane. The determinant is zero, so the
                    equilibrium is not isolated — a <em>bifurcation</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 2</m> (<m>T=4</m>, <m>D=0</m>): the curve
                    crosses the <m>T</m>-axis again. The determinant is again zero
                    — another <em>bifurcation</em>.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  The complete classification along the curve is:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \lt 0</m>: <m>T \lt 0</m>, <m>D \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>\Delta = 8\alpha \lt 0</m> — <em>stable spiral</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: <m>D = 0</m> — <em>degenerate</em>
                    (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>0 \lt \alpha \lt 2</m>: <m>T \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>D \lt 0</m> — <em>unstable saddle</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 2</m>: <m>D = 0</m> — <em>degenerate</em>
                    (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \gt 2</m>: <m>T \gt 0</m>, <m>D \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>\Delta = 8\alpha \gt 0</m> — <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  </p>
                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-11.xml"/>
                    </image>

                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    1 \amp 2 \\
                    \alpha \amp 0
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For
                  <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\alpha&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = 1, \qquad D = -2\alpha.</md>
                  As <m>\alpha</m> varies, the point <m>(T, D) = (1, -2\alpha)</m>
                  traces the <em>vertical line <m>T = 1</m></em> in the
                  trace-determinant plane, traversed downward as <m>\alpha</m>
                  increases. The discriminant along the line is
                    <md>T^2 - 4D = 1 + 8\alpha.</md>
                  </p>

                  <p>The type of equilibrium changes at the following values of
                  <m>\alpha</m>:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -\tfrac{1}{8}</m>
                    (<m>D = \tfrac{1}{4} = T^2/4</m>): the line meets the parabola
                    <m>D = T^2/4</m>; transition from <em>unstable spiral</em> to
                    <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m> (<m>D = 0</m>): the determinant
                    vanishes; the origin is not an isolated equilibrium
                    (bifurcation).</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  The complete classification is:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \lt -\tfrac{1}{8}</m>: <m>T = 1 \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>D \gt \tfrac{1}{4}</m>, <m>\Delta \lt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable spiral</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -\tfrac{1}{8}</m>: <m>D = \tfrac{1}{4}</m>,
                    <m>\Delta = 0</m> — <em>unstable improper node</em>
                    (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>-\tfrac{1}{8} \lt \alpha \lt 0</m>:
                    <m>0 \lt D \lt \tfrac{1}{4}</m>, <m>\Delta \gt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: <m>D = 0</m>
                    — <em>degenerate</em> (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \gt 0</m>: <m>D \lt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable saddle</em>.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  </p>
                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-12.xml"/>
                    </image>

                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    \alpha \amp 1 \\
                    1 \amp \alpha - 1
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For
                  <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&amp;1\\1&amp;\alpha-1\end{pmatrix}</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = 2\alpha - 1, \qquad D = \alpha(\alpha-1) - 1
                           = \alpha^2 - \alpha - 1.</md>
                  Eliminating <m>\alpha = (T+1)/2</m> gives
                    <md>D = \frac{(T+1)^2}{4} - \frac{T+1}{2} - 1
                           = \frac{T^2}{4} - \frac{5}{4},</md>
                  so the curve is a <em>downward-shifted parabola</em>
                  <m>D = T^2/4 - 5/4</m>, lying entirely below the standard parabola
                  <m>D = T^2/4</m> by <m>5/4</m>. The discriminant along the curve is
                    <md>T^2 - 4D = T^2 - 4\!\left(\frac{T^2}{4} - \frac{5}{4}\right)
                               = 5 \gt 0</md>
                  for all <m>\alpha</m>, so the eigenvalues are always real and
                  distinct; the system is never a spiral or center.
                  </p>

                  <p>The curve crosses the <m>T</m>-axis (<m>D = 0</m>) when
                  <m>T^2 = 5</m>, i.e., <m>T = \pm\sqrt{5}</m>, corresponding to
                    <md>\alpha = \frac{T+1}{2} = \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}.</md>
                  These are the two bifurcation values.
                  The complete classification is:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \lt \tfrac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2}</m>:
                    <m>T \lt -\sqrt{5}</m>, <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>\Delta \gt 0</m>
                    — <em>stable node</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = \tfrac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2}</m>: <m>D = 0</m>
                    — <em>degenerate</em> (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\tfrac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2} \lt \alpha \lt
                    \tfrac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}</m>: <m>D \lt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable saddle</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = \tfrac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}</m>: <m>D = 0</m>
                    — <em>degenerate</em> (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha \gt \tfrac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}</m>:
                    <m>T \gt \sqrt{5}</m>, <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>\Delta \gt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  </p>
                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-13.xml"/>
                    </image>


                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}
                    0 \amp 1 \\
                    \alpha \amp \sqrt{1 - \alpha^2}
                    \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="instructors">
                  <p>For
                  <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\alpha&amp;\sqrt{1-\alpha^2}
                  \end{pmatrix}</m>, defined for <m>\alpha \in [-1,1]</m>,
                  the trace and determinant are
                    <md>T = \sqrt{1-\alpha^2} \geq 0, \qquad D = -\alpha.</md>
                  Since <m>T^2 = 1 - \alpha^2</m> and <m>D = -\alpha</m>, we have
                    <md>T^2 + D^2 = (1-\alpha^2) + \alpha^2 = 1,</md>
                  so the curve is the <em>right semicircle</em> <m>T^2 + D^2 = 1</m>
                  with <m>T \geq 0</m>, traversed from <m>(T,D)=(0,1)</m> at
                  <m>\alpha=-1</m> clockwise to <m>(T,D)=(0,-1)</m> at
                  <m>\alpha=1</m>. The discriminant along the curve is
                    <md>T^2 - 4D = 1 - \alpha^2 + 4\alpha
                               = 5 - (\alpha-2)^2,</md>
                  which vanishes at <m>\alpha = 2 - \sqrt{5} \approx -0.236</m>
                  (the only root in <m>[-1,1]</m>).
                  </p>

                  <p>The type of equilibrium changes at the following values:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -1</m>: <m>T = 0</m>, <m>D = 1 \gt 0</m>
                    — <em>center</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 2-\sqrt{5} \approx -0.236</m>:
                    <m>\Delta = 0</m>, <m>T \gt 0</m>, <m>D \gt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable improper node</em> (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: <m>T = 1</m>, <m>D = 0</m>
                    — <em>degenerate</em>; not an isolated equilibrium
                    (bifurcation).</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  The complete classification along the semicircle is:
                  <ul>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = -1</m>: <m>(T,D)=(0,1)</m>
                    — <em>center</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>-1 \lt \alpha \lt 2-\sqrt{5}</m>: <m>T \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>\Delta \lt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable spiral</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 2-\sqrt{5}</m>: <m>\Delta = 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable improper node</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>2-\sqrt{5} \lt \alpha \lt 0</m>: <m>T \gt 0</m>,
                    <m>D \gt 0</m>, <m>\Delta \gt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m>: <m>D = 0</m>
                    — <em>degenerate</em> (bifurcation).</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>0 \lt \alpha \leq 1</m>: <m>D \lt 0</m>
                    — <em>unstable saddle</em>.</p></li>
                  </ul>
                  </p>

                    <image width="60%">
                     <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-14.xml"/>
                    </image>



                </solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Consider the two-parameter family of linear systems
                    <md>\begin{pmatrix} x' \\ y' \end{pmatrix}
                    =
                    \begin{pmatrix}
                    \alpha &amp; \beta \\
                    1 &amp; 0
                    \end{pmatrix}
                    \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}.</md>
                Identify all of the regions in the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane where this system possesses a saddle, a sink, a spiral sink, and so on.</p>  
            </statement>
            <solution component="instructors">
              <p>For the matrix
              <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&amp;\beta\\1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>,
              the trace and determinant are
                <md>T = \alpha, \qquad D = -\beta,</md>
              and the discriminant is <m>\Delta = T^2 - 4D = \alpha^2 + 4\beta</m>.
              The type of equilibrium at the origin is therefore determined
              entirely by the position of <m>(\alpha, \beta)</m> in the
              <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane as follows.
              </p>

              <p>The boundary curves dividing the parameter plane into regions are:
              <ul>
                <li><p><m>\beta = 0</m> (the <m>\alpha</m>-axis): <m>D = 0</m>,
                so the equilibrium is non-isolated (degenerate). For
                <m>\beta \gt 0</m> we have <m>D \lt 0</m> giving a
                <em>saddle</em>; for <m>\beta \lt 0</m> we have <m>D \gt 0</m>
                giving a node, spiral, or center.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = -\alpha^2/4</m> (downward-opening parabola):
                <m>\Delta = 0</m>, giving an <em>improper node</em>. Above this
                parabola (<m>\beta \gt -\alpha^2/4</m>) the eigenvalues are
                complex; below (<m>\beta \lt -\alpha^2/4</m>) they are real and
                distinct.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\alpha = 0</m> with <m>\beta \lt 0</m>: <m>T = 0</m>
                and <m>D \gt 0</m>, giving a <em>center</em>. This separates
                stable and unstable spirals.</p></li>
              </ul>
              </p>

              <p>The complete classification in the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane is:
              <ul>
                <li><p><m>\beta \gt 0</m>: <em>saddle</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta \lt 0</m>, <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>,
                <m>\beta \gt -\alpha^2/4</m>: <em>stable spiral</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta \lt 0</m>, <m>\alpha = 0</m>:
                <em>center</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta \lt 0</m>, <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>,
                <m>\beta \gt -\alpha^2/4</m>: <em>unstable spiral</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = -\alpha^2/4</m>, <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>:
                <em>stable improper node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = -\alpha^2/4</m>, <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>:
                <em>unstable improper node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta \lt -\alpha^2/4</m>, <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>:
                <em>stable node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta \lt -\alpha^2/4</m>, <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>:
                <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
              </ul>
              </p>
                  
                <image width="60%">
                 <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-15.xml"/>
                </image>



            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Consider the two-parameter family of linear systems
                    <md>\begin{pmatrix} x' \\ y' \end{pmatrix}
                    =
                    \begin{pmatrix}
                    \alpha &amp; \beta \\
                    \beta &amp; \alpha
                    \end{pmatrix}
                    \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}.</md>
                Identify all of the regions in the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane where this system possesses a saddle, a sink, a spiral sink, and so on.</p>  
            </statement>
            <solution component="instructors">
              <p>For the matrix
              <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&amp;\beta\\\beta&amp;\alpha\end{pmatrix}</m>,
              the trace and determinant are
                <md>T = 2\alpha, \qquad D = \alpha^2 - \beta^2,</md>
              and the discriminant is
                <md>\Delta = T^2 - 4D = 4\alpha^2 - 4(\alpha^2-\beta^2)
                         = 4\beta^2 \geq 0.</md>
              Since <m>\Delta \geq 0</m> for all <m>\alpha, \beta</m>, the
              eigenvalues are always real. In fact, the eigenvalues can be found
              directly:
                <md>\lambda_1 = \alpha + \beta, \qquad \lambda_2 = \alpha - \beta.</md>
              There are never spirals or centers; the only possible types are
              nodes, saddles, and degenerate cases.
              </p>

              <p>The boundary curves in the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane are the
              lines where one eigenvalue vanishes:
              <ul>
                <li><p><m>\lambda_1 = 0</m>: <m>\beta = -\alpha</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\lambda_2 = 0</m>: <m>\beta = \alpha</m>.</p></li>
              </ul>
              These two lines divide the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane into four
              regions. Additionally, the line <m>\beta = 0</m> (with
              <m>\alpha \neq 0</m>) gives a repeated eigenvalue
              <m>\lambda = \alpha</m> (improper node). The complete
              classification is:
              <ul>
                <li><p><m>\alpha \gt |\beta|</m> (right wedge):
                <m>\lambda_1 \gt 0</m> and <m>\lambda_2 \gt 0</m>
                — <em>unstable node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\alpha \lt -|\beta|</m> (left wedge):
                <m>\lambda_1 \lt 0</m> and <m>\lambda_2 \lt 0</m>
                — <em>stable node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>|\beta| \gt |\alpha|</m> (top and bottom wedges):
                <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m> have opposite signs
                — <em>saddle</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = 0</m>, <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>:
                repeated eigenvalue <m>\lambda = \alpha \gt 0</m>
                — <em>unstable improper node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = 0</m>, <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>:
                repeated eigenvalue <m>\lambda = \alpha \lt 0</m>
                — <em>stable improper node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = \pm\alpha</m> with <m>\alpha \neq 0</m>:
                one eigenvalue vanishes — <em>degenerate</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\alpha = \beta = 0</m>: zero matrix
                — <em>all points are equilibria</em>.</p></li>
              </ul>
              </p>

                <image width="60%">
                 <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-16.xml"/>
                </image>


            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Consider the two-parameter family of linear systems
                    <md>\begin{pmatrix} x' \\ y' \end{pmatrix}
                    =
                    \begin{pmatrix}
                    \alpha &amp; -\beta \\
                    \beta &amp; \alpha
                    \end{pmatrix}
                    \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}.</md>
                Identify all of the regions in the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane where this system possesses a saddle, a sink, a spiral sink, and so on.</p>  
            </statement>
            <solution component="instructors">
              <p>For the matrix
              <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&amp;-\beta\\\beta&amp;\alpha\end{pmatrix}</m>,
              the trace and determinant are
                <md>T = 2\alpha, \qquad D = \alpha^2 + \beta^2,</md>
              and the discriminant is
                <md>\Delta = T^2 - 4D = 4\alpha^2 - 4(\alpha^2+\beta^2)
                         = -4\beta^2 \leq 0.</md>
              The eigenvalues are
                <md>\lambda = \alpha \pm i\beta,</md>
              which are complex whenever <m>\beta \neq 0</m> and real (repeated)
              when <m>\beta = 0</m>. Since <m>D = \alpha^2 + \beta^2 \geq 0</m>
              everywhere, <em>saddles never occur</em>. The behavior is
              determined entirely by the sign of <m>\alpha</m> (the real part of
              the eigenvalue) and whether <m>\beta = 0</m>.
              </p>

              <p>The classification in the <m>\alpha\beta</m>-plane is:
              <ul>
                <li><p><m>\beta \neq 0</m>, <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>: complex
                eigenvalues with negative real part — <em>stable spiral
                (spiral sink)</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta \neq 0</m>, <m>\alpha = 0</m>: purely imaginary
                eigenvalues <m>\lambda = \pm i\beta</m> — <em>center</em>.
                Bifurcation between stable and unstable spirals.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta \neq 0</m>, <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>: complex
                eigenvalues with positive real part — <em>unstable spiral
                (spiral source)</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = 0</m>, <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>: the matrix is
                <m>\alpha I</m> with repeated negative eigenvalue — <em>stable
                star node</em>.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = 0</m>, <m>\alpha = 0</m>: zero matrix —
                every point is an equilibrium.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>\beta = 0</m>, <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>: the matrix is
                <m>\alpha I</m> with repeated positive eigenvalue — <em>unstable
                star node</em>.</p></li>
              </ul>
              Note that the classification depends only on <m>\alpha</m> (not on
              <m>|\beta|</m> for <m>\beta \neq 0</m>), so the entire left
              half-plane is a stable spiral, the right half-plane is an unstable
              spiral, and the <m>\beta</m>-axis is a center (for
              <m>\beta \neq 0</m>).
              </p>
              
            <image width="60%">
             <xi:include href="prefigure/linear07-solution-exercise-17.xml"/>
            </image>


            </solution>
        </exercise>

	</exercises>

</section>

